#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055883} :activity/id #uuid "105911a6-a8fa-5b40-bf5e-8a3845e590dc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050613 :topic/id #uuid "58d4d7b7-4b91-5e3c-ba04-7522d5cfa8f6" :topic/math-academy-id 40} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056009 :tutorial/content "Let's suppose that we want to solve an equation like \n\n$$\n\\dfrac x 6 = 4.\n$$\n\nFor this, we can use the **multiplication principle.** The multiplication principle tells us that multiplying (or dividing) both sides of an equation by the same number does not change its solution.\n\nIn this case, the variable $x$ is being *divided* by $6.$ To isolate $x$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *multiplying* by $6$. So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6.$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac x 6 \t\t\t\t\t&= 4 \\[5pt]\n\t\t{\\color{blue}{6}} \\cdot \\dfrac x 6  \t&= {\\color{blue}{6}}\\cdot 4\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{6x}{6}\t\t\t&=24\\[5pt]\n\t}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $6$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{6x}{6}\t\t\t&=24 \\\n\t\t\\dfrac{ \\cancel{6} x}{\\cancel{6} }\t\t\t&=24 \\\n\t\tx\t\t\t\t&=24\n\t}\n$$\n\n \n\nThus, our solution is $x=24.$ Let's check that this solution is correct by plugging it back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\t\\dfrac x 6 &=4 \\[5pt]\n    \t\t\\dfrac{24}{6} &=4 \\[5pt]\n\t\t4 &= 4 \\; {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n\t}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "66f28923-eb93-5bc0-b870-b4316258a3c8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8052 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "85cdcdec-1c60-5ac5-8e3f-e25e47c0bb28" :step/math-academy-id 72101 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055884}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055884 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054834 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054381 :question/id #uuid "c64cd16e-2f9d-5564-8d04-eddcad2c5d01" :question/math-academy-id "e-12859"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4007f63e-0a99-57ca-a1f6-190b21f02ecc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050414} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050599}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055443} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479855} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479893}] :knowledge-point/title "Linear Equations Requiring Multiplication by a Positive Integer"} :step/id #uuid "036eee4c-d08b-5bac-82ff-0d8314f256ca" :step/math-academy-id 72102 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054835 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054388 :question/id #uuid "cbce1708-81ba-5d37-ae21-fc8d94ebbc2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12894"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b765f7b2-639a-5519-9170-02a4fcb0f8fe" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055290} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479828} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479865}] :knowledge-point/title "Cases Where the Variable Is on the Right-Hand Side"} :step/id #uuid "f810611a-c93d-5ca0-8ad1-26bd885d89c1" :step/math-academy-id 72105 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055886}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055886 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056010 :tutorial/content "In order to remove a negative sign from our variable, we can multiply both sides by $-1$. This way, the two negatives will cancel out to form a positive. \n\nFor example, suppose that we have the equation\n\n$$\n-x = 3\n$$\n\nWe can isolate $x$ by multiplying both sides of the equation by $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x & =3 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-x) & =(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\ x & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the equation is $x=-3.$ Let's check to see that this works by substituting our solution back into the original equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-x & =3 \\\\ -(-3) & =3 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7de479d1-5bb2-5cc9-9fdc-117a02790cab" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8053 :tutorial/title "Removing a Negative Sign from a Variable"} :step/id #uuid "91e35f06-1502-52eb-91c6-d755a826ebfc" :step/math-academy-id 72108 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055887}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055887 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054836 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054382 :question/id #uuid "5fe35108-2fa8-563e-862e-ad27d4ed5334" :question/math-academy-id "e-12860"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "712bc4c6-aeaf-5081-9e64-791027b38963" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049349}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479882}] :knowledge-point/title "Linear Equations Requiring Multiplication by a Negative Integer"} :step/id #uuid "fe58020e-d340-5fac-88b2-1c50209326d9" :step/math-academy-id 72109 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055888}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055888 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056011 :tutorial/content "To solve an equation like $7x = 21$, we again need to look at what's happening to the variable and ask ourselves what the opposite operation is.\n\nIn this case, the variable $x$ is being *multiplied* by $7.$ To isolate $x$, we can perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $7$. So, we divide both sides of the equation by $7.$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t7x &= 21 \\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{7x}{{\\color{blue}7}} &=\\dfrac{21}{{\\color{blue}7}}\n\t}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $7$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t\\dfrac{7x}{{\\color{blue}7}} &=\\dfrac{21}{{\\color{blue}7}}\\[5pt]\n\t\t\\dfrac{\\cancel{7}x}{\\cancel{\\color{blue}7}} &=3 \\[5pt]\n\t\tx &=3\n\t}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's check that our answer is correct by plugging it back into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n    \t\t7x &=21 \\[5pt]\n    \t\t7(3) &=21 \\[5pt]\n\t\t21 &= 21 \\; {\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}\n\t}\n$$\n\n**Note:** If there is a negative coefficient on a variable, we can get rid of it by dividing, like we do with positive coefficients. For example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2y & =10 \\\\ \\frac{-2y}{-2} & =\\frac{10}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-2}y}{\\cancel{-2}} & =-5 \\\\ y & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1a74f63d-cfcc-5a3f-899c-6b15cfa2247f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8054 :tutorial/title "Using the Multiplication Principle to Divide"} :step/id #uuid "afb3bda0-823e-547e-bb19-4e9d28f09976" :step/math-academy-id 72112 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055889}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055889 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054837 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054383 :question/id #uuid "68865bc2-e4e2-53d9-9988-4fa962e04746" :question/math-academy-id "e-12861"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e153c18d-6f3e-5fef-a006-c5597cd88bc9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050719}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479891}] :knowledge-point/title "Linear Equations Requiring Division"} :step/id #uuid "1b859cff-8545-5c6f-9fc0-22c30fff2c3b" :step/math-academy-id 72113}] :activity/title "Solving One-Step Multiplication and Division Equations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054901}]}
